<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
	<dict>
		<!--
			Required by MSAL.

			MSAL stores its token cache in the iOS keychain, and on construction it works out the
			publisher's Team ID by writing a probe item to the keychain and reading back the access
			group iOS assigned to it. An app with no keychain-access-groups entitlement cannot do
			that, so PublicClientApplicationBuilder.Build() throws before sign-in even starts:

				MsalClientException: cannot_access_publisher_keychain
				The application cannot access the iOS keychain for the application publisher
				(the TeamId is null).

			com.microsoft.adalcache is MSAL's default group, so it needs no matching
			.WithIosKeychainSecurityGroup(...) call in code. $(AppIdentifierPrefix) is substituted
			at build time with the Team ID from the provisioning profile, which is what makes the
			group resolve to {TeamId}.com.microsoft.adalcache - the value MSAL derives on its own.

			Sharing this group is also what gives single sign-on between apps from the same
			publisher. To keep the cache private to this app instead, use
			$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier) here and pass the same value to
			.WithIosKeychainSecurityGroup(...) when building the IPublicClientApplication.

			See https://aka.ms/msal-net-enable-keychain-access
		-->
		<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
		<array>
			<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.microsoft.adalcache</string>
		</array>
	</dict>
</plist>
